  "step_3": "The decision reason hinges on the presence or absence of a sure outcome (100% probability), but neither lottery has one, so the reason would not favor either. The individual, however, does not refer to certainty or the inevitability of any outcome. Instead, they compare probabilities of loss and subjective tolerance for different loss magnitudes—factors unrelated to the presence of a sure outcome. There is no indication that the individual considered whether one of the lotteries guaranteed a particular result. The information they used (probabilities of loss, severity of loss, personal risk tolerance) does not align with the information required by the decision reason (presence of a sure outcome). Furthermore, their justification is based on a trade-off between risk and consequence, not on avoiding sure outcomes. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual applied the decision reason.",
