  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates whether either lottery contains a 'sure outcome'—that is, an outcome that occurs with 100% probability. In this decision problem, neither lottery has a sure outcome: Lottery A yields -3000 Euros with 0.2% probability and 0 Euros with 99.8% probability; Lottery B yields -6000 Euros with 0.1% probability and 0 Euros with 99.9% probability. Thus, both lotteries involve uncertainty, and neither contains a sure outcome. The decision reason would therefore be indifferent between A and B, since it only applies when at least one lottery has a sure outcome. However, the reason explicitly prefers the lottery *without* a sure outcome. Since *neither* has a sure outcome, the reason would favor both equally—though arguably, by preferring the one without a sure outcome, it would apply only when one has a sure outcome and the other does not. In this case, the reason cannot discriminate between A and B because both lack a sure outcome. Therefore, the decision reason is not applicable in a discriminatory way to this decision problem.",
