{
  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on the presence of a sure outcome (i.e., an outcome that occurs with 100% probability) in each lottery. A sure outcome would mean one of the lotteries guarantees a specific payoff regardless of chance. However, in this decision problem, neither Lottery A nor Lottery B contains a sure outcome. Lottery A has three possible outcomes: 4000 (25%), 2000 (25%), and 0 (50%). Lottery B has two outcomes: 6000 (25%) and 0 (75%). None of these outcomes occur with 100% probability. Therefore, both lotteries lack a sure outcome. The decision reason prefers the lottery *without* a sure outcome—but since neither lottery has a sure outcome, the condition for applying the reason is not met. The reason would be indifferent or undefined in this case, as both lotteries satisfy the 'no sure outcome' criterion.",
